<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kohima</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Kohima was fought from April to June 1944 at Kohima in the Naga Hills of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, as part of the Japanese offensive against India. The small British and Indian garrison held out, fighting at close quarters across the deputy commissioner's tennis court, until relieved, and the Japanese 31st Division was forced to retreat. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-imphal">Battle of Imphal</a> it marked the turning point of the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kohima.jpg" alt="Battle of Kohima" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Official photographer of No.9 Army Film and Photographic Unit. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IND_003698_Garrison_Hill_Kohima.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Japanese plan to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, codenamed U-Go, began as a spoiling attack on the British IV Corps at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imphal">Imphal</a> in Manipur. Lieutenant General Renya Mutaguchi, commanding the Fifteenth Army, enlarged it into an invasion of India itself, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-general-headquarters">Imperial General Headquarters</a> approved it on 7 January 1944. The Japanese 31st Division under Lieutenant General Kōtoku Satō was to take Kohima, which cut off Imphal's supplies, and perhaps push on to the railhead at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimapur">Dimapur</a>. Satō had misgivings and thought Mutaguchi a blockhead. His division crossed the Chindwin from 15 March with only three weeks' food and ammunition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Kohima lay at the summit of a pass and on the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimapur">Dimapur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imphal">Imphal</a>. Its ridge held the home of the Deputy Commissioner, Charles Pawsey, with its garden and tennis court. A small force of British and Indian troops, with the 1st Battalion of the Assam Regiment and the paramilitary Assam Rifles, held it when the Japanese reached the outskirts of the Naga village on 3 April. Fighting continued on the ridge until the garrison was relieved on 18 and 20 April, with some 300 wounded brought out under fire.</p><p>The British 2nd Division then counter-attacked, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> mud made the steep slopes very difficult. Japanese bunkers were deeply dug in and mutually supporting, and the progress was slow. The decisive factor was Japanese supply. By mid-May Satō's troops were starving, and a British long-range penetration brigade cut his supply lines behind him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> depended entirely on aircraft for resupply until the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimapur">Dimapur</a> was cleared.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The battle ended on 22 June when troops from Kohima and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imphal">Imphal</a> met at Milestone 109. Satō ignored army orders for several weeks and was removed in July; he refused to commit seppuku and demanded a court martial, but was declared mentally unfit to stand trial. The Japanese offensive was broken off, and the losses at Imphal and Kohima, mostly through starvation and disease, crippled their defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in the following year. The Commonwealth War Graves Commission maintains the war cemetery of 1,420 Allied dead on the old tennis court, where the Kohima Epitaph is carved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kohima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Second World War the Indian Army grew to about 2.5 million men, often called the largest volunteer army in history, and helped defeat Japan in Burma after the battles of Imphal and <b>Kohima</b> in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Indian National Army joined the failed Japanese offensive on Imphal and <b>Kohima</b> in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/state/azad-hind">Azad Hind</a></li></ul></section>
